  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter von Brüning was a German administrator and landowner from the family that helped establish the Hoechst chemical works. Trained in law at Heidelberg and Berlin, he served as district administrator of Stolp and later police president of Kiel. He also sat in the Prussian parliament and held industrial supervisory positions.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Historical reference, appointment chronology and contemporary corporate report converge on public career."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S9"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Walter von Brüning was a German administrator and landowner from the family that helped establish the Hoechst chemical works. Trained in law at Heidelberg and Berlin, he served as district administrator of Stolp and later police president of Kiel. He also sat in the Prussian parliament and held industrial supervisory positions.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter von Brüning's library included the collection of the poet Eduard Grisebach, which he had purchased. His interests ranged from German first editions and almanacs to architecture, art history and illustrated French and English literature. When his books were dispersed at auction, the second sale alone comprised some five thousand volumes.\n\nAt Semper on the island of Rügen, the German administrator also commissioned a country residence from architect Georg Steinmetz. The house and associated buildings replaced the estate's agricultural character with a landscaped residential setting. A stone-faced water tower supplied the house while resembling a medieval ruin, combining a practical function with the park's architectural design.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary bibliophile notice and regional planning association's historic estate account supply independent details."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>Walter von Brüning's library included the collection of the poet Eduard Grisebach, which he had purchased. His interests ranged from German first editions and almanacs to architecture, art history and illustrated French and English literature. When his books were dispersed at auction, the second sale alone comprised some five thousand volumes.</p>\r\n<p>At Semper on the island of Rügen, the German administrator also commissioned a country residence from architect Georg Steinmetz. The house and associated buildings replaced the estate's agricultural character with a landscaped residential setting. A stone-faced water tower supplied the house while resembling a medieval ruin, combining a practical function with the park's architectural design.</p>\r\n"
  },
